# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# Store text as LF in the repository, check out in the platform's native form.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, a contributor whose client commits CRLF
# writes blobs where EVERY line differs from the LF blobs on other branches.
# Git then cannot align the two sides, and a merge degenerates into a
# whole-file conflict even when the real change is a handful of lines. That is
# exactly what happened merging `harshit` into `test` on 2026-08-26 --
# orders/models.py, orders/views.py and orders/scheme_engine.py each came back
# as a single conflict spanning the entire file, while the true difference was
# 19 insertions and 12 deletions. Merging with `-Xrenormalize` made all three
# merge cleanly with no conflicts at all.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect binary content and leave it untouched, so this is
# safe as a blanket rule.
* text=auto

# Explicit rules where the tool cares about the ending regardless of platform.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# cmd.exe requires CRLF in batch files.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Never munge these.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.rpt   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
